Install the DLL to BINDIR

install(TARGETS) names only the unnamed DESTINATION and LIBRARY DESTINATION,
both CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR. On ELF platforms that is the whole story, because a
shared library is a single LIBRARY artifact. On Windows a SHARED target is two
artifacts -- a RUNTIME .dll and an ARCHIVE import .dll.a -- and with no RUNTIME
destination given CMake falls back to the unnamed DESTINATION, so the DLL is
installed next to the import library in lib/:

    ucrt64/lib/libqtermwidget6.dll
    ucrt64/lib/libqtermwidget6.dll.a

Windows has no rpath and resolves DLLs from PATH, which contains bin and not
lib, so nothing linked against the library can start. Name all three artifact
kinds explicitly. The unnamed DESTINATION is dropped rather than left in place:
once RUNTIME/LIBRARY/ARCHIVE are all given it only serves to catch a fourth
artifact kind by accident.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -294,9 +294,10 @@
 )
 
 install(TARGETS ${QTERMWIDGET_LIBRARY_NAME}
-    DESTINATION "${CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR}"
     EXPORT "${QTERMWIDGET_LIBRARY_NAME}-targets"
+    RUNTIME DESTINATION "${CMAKE_INSTALL_BINDIR}"
     LIBRARY DESTINATION "${CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR}"
+    ARCHIVE DESTINATION "${CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR}"
     PUBLIC_HEADER
     COMPONENT Runtime
 )
